  abstract = {Publisher Summary This chapter defines vectors and describes their algebraic and geometric properties. It also introduces another fundamental object---matrix---whose basic properties parallel those of the vector and discusses the examination of techniques that are useful for reading and writing proofs. The link between algebraic manipulation and geometric intuition is a recurring theme in linear algebra that is used to establish many important results. The study of linear algebra begins with vectors and matrices---two of the most practical concepts in mathematics. Linear algebra, in addition to having a multitude of practical applications in science and engineering, also can be used to introduce proof-writing skills. The concept of proof is central to higher mathematics. Mathematicians claim no statement as a ``fact'' until it is proven true using logical deduction. Therefore, no one can succeed in higher mathematics without mastering the techniques required to supply such a proof.},
